=============================================================
  Long-Run Gain and Mean Lag, in Levels
=============================================================

  gain     = lim_k G_k, the PERMANENT effect of a one-s.d. shock
  mean lag = sum k delta_k / sum delta_k, with delta_k the increment of
             the response: how long the level takes to get there
  converged at k = 38 (largest increment 6.37e-11)

Structural shock standard deviations (one-s.d. shock size):
  muskrat      : 0.279226
  mink         : 0.305980

Shock to muskrat:
  muskrat      : gain =   0.018776   mean lag =  -46.45 periods
  mink         : gain =   0.083916   mean lag =   -3.24 periods

Shock to mink:
  muskrat      : gain =   0.071425   mean lag =   12.80 periods
  mink         : gain =   0.319213   mean lag =    0.93 periods

  self-check  max |beta' gain| = 1.758e-10
  beta'Y_t is stationary, so no shock can move it permanently: this is
  zero by construction, and it exercises the weights, the level map and
  the orthogonalisation at once.  The gain matrix has rank M - r = 1.


